{
  "image_name": "02ddaee322170cd0aaffbbe81d31e1b5.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how the timing of a mutation during development determines how widely it is distributed in the body and whether it can be inherited. The left side shows normal human development from fertilization through zygote, gastrula, embryo, fetus, and adult. The other columns compare three mutation scenarios.\n\nIf a mutation is present in the germline or arises very early around fertilization, it becomes incorporated into essentially all cells of the developing individual, so the entire adult is shown as uniformly affected. A mutation during gamete formation or a de novo mutation in the zygote therefore produces a constitutional mutation carried throughout the body.\n\nBy contrast, mutations that occur after development has begun lead to somatic mosaicism. Because the mutation arises in only one cell lineage, only a subset of tissues is affected, shown as patchy or regional red areas in the embryo, fetus, and adult. The earlier this post-zygotic event happens, the larger the affected fraction appears.\n\nFinally, a mutation arising later in somatic life remains even more restricted, limited to a small tissue region or organ. Overall, the figure highlights the relationship between developmental timing, lineage spread, and whole-body versus mosaic distribution of mutations.",
